{"study_accession":"PRJNA562932","secondary_study_accession":"SRP219660","title":"Repeat-element-aware profiling of plasma cell-free RNA","description":"Liquid biopsies provide a means for the profiling of cell-free RNAs secreted by cells throughout the body. Although well-annotated coding and noncoding transcripts in blood are readily detectable and can serve as biomarkers of disease, the overall diagnostic utility of the cell-free transcriptome remains unclear. Here we show that transposable elements and other repeat RNA elements are enriched in the cell-free transcriptome of patients with cancer, and that they serve as signatures for the accurate classification of the disease. We used repeat-element-aware sequencing and analysis technology and single-molecule nanopore sequencing to profile the cell-free transcriptome in plasma from patients with cancer and to examine millions of genomic features comprised of all annotated genes and of repeat elements throughout the genome. By aggregating individual repeat elements to the subfamily level, we found that samples with pancreatic cancer are enriched with specific Alu subfamilies, whereas other cancers exhibit their own characteristic cell-free RNA profile. Our findings show that repetitive RNA sequences are abundant in blood and can be used as disease-specific diagnostic biomarkers. Overall design: 32 total (N = 10 Pancreatic Cancer, N = 22 Normal healthy donors) blood plasma samples were sequenced to determine the potential for disease classification from cell-free RNA","pubmed":"37652985","geo_series":"","n_samples":34,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN36307540","study_accession":"PRJNA562932","blood_class":"plasma_serum_cfrna","class_from":"sample","title":"panc, IV, 65, 1","description":"panc, IV, 65, 1","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7548897","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2023-09-02","attributes":[["source_name","Blood Plasma"],["tissue","Blood Plasma"],["subject status","pancreatic cancer pa